rules_version = '2';

// Holland Cookie — Firestore security rules.
//
// DENY BY DEFAULT, AND DENY ABSOLUTELY.
//
// This is not a placeholder and it is not a stricter-than-necessary default that
// somebody is expected to relax later. It is the correct and final rule set for
// this system, because *no client ever talks to Firestore*.
//
// The storefront and the dashboard are both plain browser apps that speak only
// to the Express API over HTTPS. Neither one loads the Firebase Web SDK; neither
// one holds a Firebase credential; neither one knows a project id. Every read
// and every write to this database originates from `backend/` using the Firebase
// Admin SDK with a service account.
//
// The Admin SDK bypasses these rules entirely. That is the point worth being
// explicit about, because it is the thing that gets misunderstood: these rules
// do NOT protect the API. They cannot. Every authorization decision that matters
// — is this caller an admin, may this customer read this order, may this field
// be written — is enforced in Express before the Admin SDK is ever called, in
// `requireAdmin`, `requireCustomer`, and the per-route Zod schemas.
//
// What these rules DO protect is the case where a project id and a leaked or
// guessed client API key are used to reach Firestore directly, from outside our
// server, over the public REST/gRPC endpoints. Against that, the answer is no,
// to everything, always.
//
// If a genuine direct-from-client feature is ever added, it gets its own narrow
// allow block with its own rules tests. Until then, anything other than a total
// denial here is a mistake.

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
